<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jena</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jena is a university city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saale">Saale</a> river in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. Its university, founded in 1558, made it a centre of German philosophy and literature around 1800. On 14 October 1806 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> defeated the Prussians near the city at the Battle of Jena. From the nineteenth century Carl <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carl-zeiss-ag">Zeiss</a> and Otto Schott made Jena a centre of the optical and glass industries.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jena.jpg" alt="Jena" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Henry Mühlpfordt · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zentrum_Jenas_im_Tal_2008-05-24.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Jena lies in a hilly landscape in the east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a>, in the wide valley of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saale">Saale</a> river. The city centre stands at about 160 metres, and the hills on both sides of the valley rise to about 400 metres. Until the High Middle Ages the Saale was the border between Germanic regions in the west and Slavic regions in the east. Jena was a river crossing, but its relevance in the Middle Ages was local.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The first unequivocal mention of Jena is in a document of 1182, and its first local rulers were the Lords of Lobdeburg. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Protestant Reformation</a> reached it in 1523, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther">Martin Luther</a> visited to reorganise church affairs, and the townsmen attacked the Carmelite and Dominican convents, which were abolished in 1525 and 1548. The city remained a small town until the 19th century.</p><p>Around 1790 the university became the largest and most prominent among the German states. It made Jena the centre of idealist philosophy, with professors such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-gottlieb-fichte">Johann Gottlieb Fichte</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georg-wilhelm-friedrich-hegel">Georg Wilhelm Friedrich Hegel</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friedrich-schiller">Friedrich Schiller</a> and Friedrich Wilhelm Joseph Schelling, and gave rise to Jena Romanticism, the first phase of German Romanticism, with a group led by Caroline Schlegel and poets such as Novalis, August and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friedrich-schlegel">Friedrich Schlegel</a> and Ludwig Tieck. Goethe and Schiller met at the university in 1794 and formed a long friendship.</p><p>On 14 October 1806 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> defeated the Prussian army near the district of Vierzehnheiligen in the Battle of Jena-Auerstedt. Resistance to the French occupation was strong, especially among the students, many of whom fought in the Lützow Free Corps in 1813, and the Urburschenschaft fraternity was founded in the city two years later. Industrialisation then changed the social structure: the former academic town became a working-class city, and its population rose from 8,000 around 1870 to 71,000 at the beginning of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>. The optical and glass industry, around companies such as Carl Zeiss and Schott, grew steadily, and Jena was a world centre of the optical industry for most of the 20th century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Jena is the second largest city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a>, with about 110,000 inhabitants. The Friedrich Schiller University, founded in 1558, remains the heart of its education and research. Its main church, St Michael's, built between 1422 and 1557, is one of the biggest Gothic monuments in Thuringia, and the botanic garden, established in 1794, is one of the oldest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jena” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>When war between Prussia and Napoleon broke out in 1806, Saxony sided with Prussia, and its army shared in the defeat at <b>Jena</b> in October 1806.</blockquote><a href="/empire/electorate-of-saxony">Electorate of Saxony · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In <b>Jena</b> a circle formed around the brothers August Wilhelm and Friedrich Schlegel, with Caroline Schlegel, Dorothea Veit, the poet Novalis, Ludwig Tieck and the philosophers Fichte and Schelling, and their journal Athenaeum appeared in Berlin from 1798 to 1800.</blockquote><a href="/movement/romanticism">Romanticism · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>Edward Chiera published further tablets in 1924 and 1934, and others were found in the Louvre, the Ashmolean in Oxford, the Hilprecht collection at <b>Jena</b>, Geneva, Berlin, Yale, the Nippur collection in Istanbul, and at Ur itself in Charles Woolley's excavations.</blockquote><a href="/songs/lament-for-ur">Lament for Ur</a></li><li><blockquote>Emperor Napoleon entered Berlin without a fight, three days after French forces had captured the Prussian capital following their victory at <b>Jena</b>–Auerstedt.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-27">27 October in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The duchy took in parts of Saxe-<b>Jena</b> in 1690.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-saxe-eisenach">Duchy of Saxe-Eisenach</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 1680 division of the inheritance of Ernest the Pious, his fifth son Christian received Eisenberg and its district, between Gera and <b>Jena</b>, founding the Duchy of Saxe-Eisenberg.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-saxe-eisenberg">Duchy of Saxe-Eisenberg</a></li></ul></section>
